# The Wi-Fi keepalive drop test (RC_JOBS=rc.jobs.wifidrop). sleep_hold keeps the system from
# idle-sleeping for the run: without it the whole job is suspended a minute in (a scene cannot
# wake a sleeping phone), which is what stopped the earlier attempts (logs/064236-run.log,
# logs/065221-run.log). The background rwtouch lines keep the display from dimming while the
# foreground joins, then takes the link away on purpose (net_test leave). The kernel posts the
# link-off event; com.hakios.wifi-keepalive reads it, waits five seconds to tell a real loss
# from a join's own bounce, and joins /redwood/fw/network again. The last lines ask for the
# address and a name: they are the proof that the link came back by itself.
/redwood/bin/sleep_hold 400 &
/redwood/bin/rwtouch wait 5 tap 0.5 0.5 wait 5 tap 0.5 0.5 wait 5 tap 0.5 0.5 wait 5 tap 0.5 0.5 wait 5 tap 0.5 0.5 wait 5 tap 0.5 0.5 &
/redwood/bin/rwtouch wait 40 tap 0.5 0.5 wait 5 tap 0.5 0.5 wait 5 tap 0.5 0.5 wait 5 tap 0.5 0.5 wait 5 tap 0.5 0.5 wait 5 tap 0.5 0.5 &
/redwood/bin/rwtouch wait 75 tap 0.5 0.5 wait 5 tap 0.5 0.5 wait 5 tap 0.5 0.5 wait 5 tap 0.5 0.5 wait 5 tap 0.5 0.5 wait 5 tap 0.5 0.5 &
/redwood/bin/rwtouch wait 110 tap 0.5 0.5 wait 5 tap 0.5 0.5 wait 5 tap 0.5 0.5 wait 5 tap 0.5 0.5 wait 5 tap 0.5 0.5 wait 5 tap 0.5 0.5 &
/redwood/bin/net_test wait 30 joinfile /redwood/fw/network
/redwood/bin/net_test waitaddr en0 60 resolve apt.procurs.us
/redwood/bin/net_test leave
/redwood/bin/net_test wait 10
/redwood/bin/net_test waitaddr en0 60
/redwood/bin/net_test resolve apt.procurs.us
